12
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
Blood Diamond is a 2006 American political war thriller film directed and co-produced by Edward
Zwick and starring Leonardo DiCaprio, Jennifer Connelly, and Djimon Hounsou.

13
01:17:27,500 --> 01:17:33,500
The title refers to blood diamonds, which are diamonds mined in war zones and sold to finance
conflicts, and thereby profit warlords and diamond companies around the world.

14
01:23:25,000 --> 01:23:31,000
Set during the Sierra Leone Civil War of 1991–2002, the film depicts a country torn
apart by the struggle between government loyalists and insurgent forces.

15
01:29:22,500 --> 01:29:28,500
It also portrays many of the atrocities of that war, including the rebels' amputation of
civilians' hands to discourage them from voting in upcoming elections.

16
01:35:20,000 --> 01:35:26,000
The film's ending, in which a conference is held concerning blood diamonds, refers to a
historic meeting that took place in Kimberley, South Africa, in 2000.

17
01:41:17,500 --> 01:41:23,500
It led to development of the Kimberley Process Certification Scheme, which sought to certify the origin of rough diamonds in order
to curb the trade in conflict diamonds; the certification scheme has since been mostly abandoned as ineffective.

18
01:47:15,000 --> 01:47:21,000
The film received positive reviews, with praise directed
toward the performances of DiCaprio and Hounsou.

19
01:53:12,500 --> 01:53:18,500
The film grossed $171 million worldwide and received five Oscar nominations,
including Best Actor for DiCaprio and Best Supporting Actor for Hounsou.

20
01:59:10,000 --> 01:59:16,000
DiCaprio received a nomination for a Golden Globe Award for Best Actor – Motion Picture
Drama (also nominated that year in the same category for The Departed).

21
02:05:07,500 --> 02:05:13,500
In addition, DiCaprio and Hounsou were nominated for Outstanding Male Actor in a Leading Role and
Outstanding Male Actor in a Supporting Role at the 13th Screen Actors Guild Awards.

22
02:11:05,000 --> 02:11:11,000
The film explores themes of bootlegger, journalist,
smuggling (contraband), loss of loved one, africa.

23
02:17:02,500 --> 02:17:08,500
The tone is intense and dark and emotional.
